This PR fixes the `color-mix(…)` polyfill for theme colors with escaped characters in their name.

The polyfill looks up the variable of a `var(…)` in the theme to inline its color in the fallback. The value parser keeps CSS escapes in the variable name (e.g. `--color-brand\.light`), but the theme stores its keys unescaped (`--color-brand.light`). So the lookup missed, and the fallback lost its opacity:

```css
@theme {
  --color-brand\.light: oklch(63.7% 0.237 25.331);
}
@tailwind utilities;
```

`text-brand.light/50` generated:

```css
.text-brand\.light\/50 {
  color: var(--color-brand\.light);
}
```

Now it generates:

```css
.text-brand\.light\/50 {
  color: #fb2c3680;
}
```

The fix unescapes the variable name before the lookup, as `Theme#markUsedVariable` and `Theme#getOptions` already do.

## Conflict with https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/pull/20606

https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/pull/20606 changes the same line, to `designSystem.theme.unprefixKey(variableName)`. Whichever PR merges second has a small conflict there. The final form is:

```ts
designSystem.theme.resolveValue(null, [unescape(designSystem.theme.unprefixKey(variableName)) as any])
```

## Test plan

- Added a test with `--color-brand\.light` and `--color-1\.5: var(--color-brand\.light)`, which also covers an escaped name in a nested `var(…)`. It fails without the fix.
- `pnpm vitest run` in `packages/tailwindcss` passes.

**What version of Tailwind CSS are you using?**

v4.3.5 (also v4.3.1 to v4.3.4)

**What build tool (or framework if it abstracts the build tool) are you using?**

`@tailwindcss/cli`, and `compile()` of `tailwindcss`

**Reproduction**

```css
@import "tailwindcss";

@mixin --card(--bg, --pad) {
  background: var(--bg);
  padding: var(--pad);
}

.a {
  @apply --card(red, 2rem);
}
```

**Describe your issue**

Since 4.3.1, `@apply` with only dashed identifiers is a CSS mixin, and it stays in the output (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-mixins-1/#apply-rule). But the stylesheet above gives this error:

```
Error: You cannot use `@apply` with both mixins and utilities. Please move `@apply --card(red,` into a separate rule.
```

`substituteAtApply` in `packages/tailwindcss/src/apply.ts` splits the params at each space (`child.params.split(/(\s+)/g)`). The space after the comma in the arguments makes two parts: `--card(red,` (a dashed identifier) and `2rem)` (a utility). The same occurs with an argument in braces, as the spec gives for a value with commas: `@apply --card({ 1px, 2px }, 3px)`.

With no space (`@apply --card(red,2rem)`), the mixin stays, as it must.

**Expected**

The output has `@apply --card(red, 2rem);`, with no error.

**A possible fix**

Before the check for dashed identifiers, split the params only at the spaces that are not in parentheses, brackets, braces or strings (for example with `segment(params, ' ')`, and with no empty parts). The check then sees one part, `--card(red, 2rem)`. For a list of utilities, the parts are the same as now, because a candidate has no space in brackets or parentheses, so the result does not change where there is no error now.
